WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:04.740
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:06.060
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:07.340
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:08.600
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:12.420
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:16.780
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes

7
00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:19.400
stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

8
00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:20.540
We're The Office Ladies.

9
00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:24.620
Angela Kinsey.

10
00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:25.660
Jenna Fisher.

11
00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:27.680
I think I have a meta moment.

12
00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:35.560
Because today, in the world, the 100th episode of Office Ladies podcast came out.

13
00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:36.120
Yes.

14
00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:41.980
And now here we are recording our podcast about the 100th episode of The Office.

15
00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:43.460
That's very meta.

16
00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:45.620
And our ages almost equal 100.

17
00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:48.500
Oh, well, why'd you have to throw that in there?

18
00:00:48.580 --> 00:00:49.020
I don't know.

19
00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:50.400
I was looking for more hundreds.

20
00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:53.740
All right.

21
00:00:53.740 --> 00:00:55.500
Well, we should get into this episode.

22
00:00:55.620 --> 00:00:57.200
We have so much to cover.

23
00:00:57.420 --> 00:00:57.900
We do.

24
00:00:58.160 --> 00:00:59.600
This is going to be a long one.

25
00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:00.740
That's what she said.

26
00:01:00.740 --> 00:01:08.140
It's Company Picnic, Season 5, Episode 28, written by Jen Salata and Paul Lieberstein,

27
00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:12.740
directed by the dearest man on the planet, Ken Kwapis.

28
00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:13.880
Summary.

29
00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:17.440
It's the Dunder Mifflin Company Picnic.

30
00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:19.200
All of them are there.

31
00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:20.180
All the branches.

32
00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:20.760
Yes.

33
00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:24.660
In their own special t-shirts that have their branch name on them.

34
00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:25.780
Oh, I have it, lady.

35
00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:26.540
We'll get to it.

36
00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:31.720
Well, the Scranton branch is going to compete against the other branches in a company-wide

37
00:01:31.720 --> 00:01:32.960
volleyball tournament.

38
00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:39.760
Meanwhile, Michael and Holly reunite to perform a comedy skit where they accidentally reveal

39
00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:43.220
some confidential information to the crowd.

40
00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:49.140
David Wallace, why would you tell Michael Scott anything of that magnitude and expect him

41
00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:49.920
to keep it quiet?

42
00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:57.460
When David introduces their skit, one of my favorite lines is when he says, I have not seen

43
00:01:57.460 --> 00:01:57.600
this.

44
00:01:57.660 --> 00:01:58.440
I have not seen this.

45
00:01:58.560 --> 00:01:58.660
Yeah.

46
00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:05.200
Finally, Dwight is going to defend Angela's honor.

47
00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:06.600
Tell Rolf.

48
00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:07.440
Yeah.

49
00:02:07.720 --> 00:02:07.940
Yeah.

50
00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:13.980
And ultimately, an ankle injury is going to send Pam to the hospital where she and Jim

51
00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:15.340
get some surprising news.

52
00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:16.440
So sweet.

53
00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:17.720
I love this episode.

54
00:02:18.140 --> 00:02:24.200
Fast fact number one, Company Picnic was the 100th episode of The Office.

55
00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:25.220
Yes.

56
00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:31.360
This was a very big deal for the network because it meant our show was now eligible for syndication.

57
00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:33.300
Which I didn't even know what that meant.

58
00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:35.160
I thought maybe we should explain it.

59
00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:35.580
Okay.

60
00:02:35.780 --> 00:02:35.960
Do.

61
00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:39.040
Because I remember when we hit 100, I was like, what's all the hullabaloo?

62
00:02:39.140 --> 00:02:39.540
Yeah, sure.

63
00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:40.060
We hit 100.

64
00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:40.580
That's cool.

65
00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:41.020
I know.

66
00:02:41.020 --> 00:02:43.900
Maybe, you know, we'll all go out to dinner or something.

67
00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:44.660
No.

68
00:02:44.740 --> 00:02:44.980
No.

69
00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:46.940
For the network, super big.

70
00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:48.540
They all showed up to the set.

71
00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:49.300
They did.

72
00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:51.140
They brought a cake.

73
00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:54.000
It was as big as like a poker table.

74
00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:55.460
It was humongous.

75
00:02:55.540 --> 00:02:55.980
It was.

76
00:02:56.100 --> 00:02:56.680
That's what she said.

77
00:02:56.940 --> 00:02:57.940
That's what she said.

78
00:02:58.040 --> 00:02:58.940
They brought champagne.

79
00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:00.500
But we couldn't drink it.

80
00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:03.160
No, because we had to go back to work.

81
00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:07.360
We actually only had 15 minutes with the cake, all said and told.

82
00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:08.760
It was on our lunch break.

83
00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:12.960
So if you wanted to eat, we had 30 minutes, photo with a cake, and then we had to like

84
00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:13.680
run out the door.

85
00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:16.700
Well, I remember Greg gave a very nice speech.

86
00:03:16.820 --> 00:03:17.140
He did.

87
00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:19.380
About what the 100 episodes had meant to him.

88
00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:20.740
Entertainment Tonight was there.

89
00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:21.940
All the press was there.

90
00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:23.320
I mean, this was a super big deal.

91
00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:25.200
Let's tell you why.

92
00:03:26.140 --> 00:03:31.720
When a show hits 100 episodes and goes into syndication, it means that the network can

93
00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:38.040
now start selling the show in a package to different places where they can rerun it.

94
00:03:38.180 --> 00:03:41.500
So it doesn't just run on NBC anymore.

95
00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:43.960
It gets on all the cable networks.

96
00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:46.760
Like Comedy Central, how it reruns on Comedy Central.

97
00:03:46.940 --> 00:03:47.260
Right.

98
00:03:47.540 --> 00:03:51.080
And guys, remember, this predates all streaming.

99
00:03:51.380 --> 00:03:51.900
Yes.

100
00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:54.160
So this was all kind of a cable thing.

101
00:03:54.380 --> 00:03:57.160
But this meant big money for the network.

102
00:03:57.340 --> 00:04:01.140
I didn't know that, but it makes sense because these other places pay them

103
00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:02.120
to have it.

104
00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:02.560
Yes.

105
00:04:02.860 --> 00:04:08.980
But what I think a lot of people don't know is that the actors do not cash in on this

106
00:04:08.980 --> 00:04:12.000
unless they are what is called profit participants.

107
00:04:12.340 --> 00:04:12.660
Oh, yeah.

108
00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:13.220
And guess what?

109
00:04:13.300 --> 00:04:13.720
We weren't.

110
00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:14.420
We were not.

111
00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:20.300
Now, our union, SAG, does negotiate a small fee for syndicated reruns.

112
00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:22.560
So it's very fun.

113
00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:24.180
You get checks, and they're great.

114
00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:24.900
They're great.

115
00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:27.140
But they have, you guys, this like cascading tier.

116
00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:28.540
So you get a check, and you're like, woo-boo.

117
00:04:28.840 --> 00:04:29.960
Then the next one's a little lower.

118
00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:30.880
Then it's a little lower.

119
00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:31.940
Then it's a little lower.

120
00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:32.940
Then it's a little lower.

121
00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:33.960
Then it's a little lower.

122
00:04:34.180 --> 00:04:34.600
Yes.

123
00:04:34.760 --> 00:04:35.780
And then it's for like $2.

124
00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:40.400
Well, you know that bar that they have called Residuals in Burbank?

125
00:04:40.660 --> 00:04:42.220
This is a famous bar, you guys.

126
00:04:42.420 --> 00:04:42.600
Yeah.

127
00:04:42.680 --> 00:04:44.520
So there's this bar called Residuals.

128
00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:49.560
And if you're an actor, and you bring in a residual check that's under $1, they'll give

129
00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:52.820
you a free drink, and then they tack your check up on the wall.

130
00:04:53.020 --> 00:04:53.260
Yeah.

131
00:04:53.260 --> 00:04:59.560
I have a friend of mine who saved a residual check for, I think it was $0.17, and like put

132
00:04:59.560 --> 00:05:00.640
it on his bulletin board.

133
00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:02.540
Lady, I got one for $0.21.

134
00:05:02.840 --> 00:05:04.660
It costs more money to mail it.

135
00:05:04.760 --> 00:05:05.200
I know.

136
00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:05.620
To me.

137
00:05:06.220 --> 00:05:06.620
Probably.

138
00:05:06.860 --> 00:05:07.580
And to print it.

139
00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:08.180
Yeah.

140
00:05:08.360 --> 00:05:08.540
Yeah.

141
00:05:09.140 --> 00:05:09.540
Exactly.

142
00:05:09.880 --> 00:05:13.260
So yeah, they were able to make a lot of money selling the show to cable.

143
00:05:13.480 --> 00:05:17.420
And then eventually, you guys, I mean, you know, they were able to sell it to all the

144
00:05:17.420 --> 00:05:17.940
streamers.

145
00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:18.460
Yeah.

146
00:05:18.460 --> 00:05:23.340
I guess we should probably say the actors don't get a big payout for that either.

147
00:05:23.580 --> 00:05:26.220
That money goes to the profit participants.

148
00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:29.960
See, the word profit participant is something I'd never heard of.

149
00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:35.260
And as it turns out, if you're one of those folks, that is the shiitake.

150
00:05:35.780 --> 00:05:36.340
Mm-hmm.

151
00:05:36.680 --> 00:05:37.160
Mm-hmm.

152
00:05:37.480 --> 00:05:37.960
Mm-hmm.

153
00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:38.840
It sure is.

154
00:05:39.340 --> 00:05:45.680
Angela, you found out the inspiration for our 100th episode while listening to the DVD

155
00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:46.380
commentary.

156
00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:47.740
Will you share?

157
00:05:47.740 --> 00:05:48.560
I will.

158
00:05:48.700 --> 00:05:51.560
I'm officially part of Fast Fact number one, folks.

159
00:05:51.640 --> 00:05:52.020
Oh, yeah.

160
00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:52.960
Here's the thing.

161
00:05:53.100 --> 00:05:58.480
If you love really good DVD commentaries, this is one not to miss of the Office box set

162
00:05:58.480 --> 00:06:03.340
because Ken Quapis and Paul Lieberstein and Jen Salata share so many great details.

163
00:06:03.540 --> 00:06:08.500
They are the only three people on this commentary, and I really enjoyed it.

164
00:06:08.820 --> 00:06:13.300
Paul shared that the idea for the company picnic, they had it at the very beginning of

165
00:06:13.300 --> 00:06:13.900
season five.

166
00:06:14.120 --> 00:06:17.240
They always knew that they would be working towards this episode.

167
00:06:17.650 --> 00:06:17.980
Wow.

168
00:06:18.700 --> 00:06:22.240
And Jen shared that they wanted to bring all of the branches together.

169
00:06:22.580 --> 00:06:24.780
They researched company picnics.

170
00:06:25.020 --> 00:06:29.520
This delighted me because I just imagined Jen Googling company picnics.

171
00:06:29.700 --> 00:06:31.260
She was a lady who Googled.

172
00:06:31.260 --> 00:06:33.560
I was going to say, like, that would have been us.

173
00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:36.560
We would have been like, wait, let's research what people do.

174
00:06:36.920 --> 00:06:39.720
And one of the things that came up was a volleyball tournament.

175
00:06:39.880 --> 00:06:41.440
So they thought that would be fun.

176
00:06:41.780 --> 00:06:45.940
And Paul said he thought it was a great way to bring everyone back together, all the different

177
00:06:45.940 --> 00:06:49.940
branches, all the different characters for this big 100th episode.

178
00:06:49.940 --> 00:06:55.340
The only characters you don't see that were in the world of the show, really, were Jan.

179
00:06:55.580 --> 00:06:57.440
But it doesn't make sense for Jan to come back.

180
00:06:57.600 --> 00:06:57.880
Yes.

181
00:06:58.060 --> 00:06:58.660
And Karen.

182
00:06:58.940 --> 00:07:00.460
Yes, we got mail about that.

183
00:07:00.460 --> 00:07:01.380
Where was Karen?

184
00:07:01.980 --> 00:07:08.780
And Jen Salata did an interview where she said Rashida Jones was filming Parks and Recreation,

185
00:07:08.780 --> 00:07:12.580
and they couldn't get her for a whole week of work to be at this picnic.

186
00:07:12.860 --> 00:07:16.920
And then they realized, wait, she would be on maternity leave.

187
00:07:17.040 --> 00:07:21.780
So they tracked her story-wise as being on maternity leave, and that's why she wasn't

188
00:07:21.780 --> 00:07:21.980
there.

189
00:07:21.980 --> 00:07:23.440
Well, that worked out perfect.

190
00:07:24.220 --> 00:07:28.760
While the volleyball was a great idea, Ken did share that it was a logistical nightmare

191
00:07:28.760 --> 00:07:33.440
because they started filming, and they were trying to keep with the rules of volleyball,

192
00:07:33.660 --> 00:07:39.200
which means the teams trade sides depending on score or whatever, but they just had to

193
00:07:39.200 --> 00:07:39.520
bail.

194
00:07:39.940 --> 00:07:42.580
They had to bail because it just got too complicated.

195
00:07:43.360 --> 00:07:48.060
And Jen said she had this memory of looking over at Ken, and he had a clipboard, and he

196
00:07:48.060 --> 00:07:54.720
had a little quarter, and he was tracing this coin with a pencil and placing it and mapping

197
00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:59.500
out where everyone was going to stand and who was serving and who was playing what position

198
00:07:59.500 --> 00:08:02.400
because it was so complex to shoot.

199
00:08:02.800 --> 00:08:06.640
I thought it was interesting that they chose volleyball.

200
00:08:07.080 --> 00:08:13.000
Any company picnic I've ever been to, it's usually softball, but I do see how people are

201
00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:16.120
really spread out in softball, so I guess that makes sense to me.

202
00:08:16.360 --> 00:08:16.580
Right.

203
00:08:16.580 --> 00:08:18.600
But, I mean, I can't even imagine.

204
00:08:18.940 --> 00:08:20.400
Jen and Paul shared this other thing.

205
00:08:20.620 --> 00:08:25.720
Jen said that her and Paul, as they wrote this episode, they thought that each team had

206
00:08:25.720 --> 00:08:30.660
nine players, and so they wrote the whole script with each team having nine players.

207
00:08:30.860 --> 00:08:31.300
Oh, no.

208
00:08:31.420 --> 00:08:35.260
And then they got to the production meeting, and in the production meeting, someone was

209
00:08:35.260 --> 00:08:40.120
like, you know, on volleyball teams, you have six players on the court, not nine.

210
00:08:40.280 --> 00:08:41.560
And Paul and them were like, what?

211
00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:44.660
Gosh.

212
00:08:44.920 --> 00:08:47.080
But, yeah, so they had to change that immediately.

213
00:08:47.080 --> 00:08:51.360
And I have more fun tidbits from the three of them in the commentary I'll sprinkle through

214
00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:51.880
the episode.

215
00:08:52.120 --> 00:08:52.660
All right.

216
00:08:52.940 --> 00:08:53.380
Angela.

217
00:08:53.600 --> 00:08:53.860
What?

218
00:08:54.040 --> 00:08:55.180
Great job on that fast fact.

219
00:08:55.320 --> 00:08:56.080
Oh, thank you.

220
00:08:56.140 --> 00:08:56.920
I really enjoyed that.

221
00:08:57.320 --> 00:08:58.060
Thanks so much.

222
00:08:58.140 --> 00:08:58.560
You told me.

223
00:08:58.700 --> 00:09:02.180
You were like, Jenna, there's some great stuff about the inspiration behind the episode,

224
00:09:02.180 --> 00:09:05.440
and I said, I'm holding a spot for you up in fast facts, lady.

225
00:09:05.700 --> 00:09:05.940
You did.

226
00:09:06.520 --> 00:09:07.020
All right.

227
00:09:07.220 --> 00:09:11.320
Fast fact number two is a location breakdown.

228
00:09:11.320 --> 00:09:17.980
Now, the signs hanging in this episode say that this picnic is being held at the Meadows

229
00:09:17.980 --> 00:09:19.480
in upstate New York.

230
00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:24.300
I could not find any information on this, or if this is a real place.

231
00:09:24.520 --> 00:09:24.760
Hmm.

232
00:09:25.220 --> 00:09:26.020
Fakie place.

233
00:09:26.360 --> 00:09:26.920
Perhaps.

234
00:09:27.320 --> 00:09:28.080
Does anyone know?

235
00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:33.480
Please comment on Office Ladies Pod if you've ever been to a park called the Meadows

236
00:09:33.480 --> 00:09:34.400
in upstate New York.

237
00:09:34.500 --> 00:09:35.100
I'd love to know.

238
00:09:35.400 --> 00:09:36.380
Fan question.

239
00:09:36.380 --> 00:09:42.240
Rayyan from Pakistan would like to know, where did we actually film this episode?

240
00:09:42.500 --> 00:09:46.200
Well, we filmed at Kalamigos Ranch in Malibu Canyon.

241
00:09:46.460 --> 00:09:49.140
It is a beautiful location.

242
00:09:49.140 --> 00:09:53.960
It's kind of nestled into the side of this canyon in the Malibu Mountains.

243
00:09:54.460 --> 00:09:59.760
Did you know it has its own water source from a spring on the property?

244
00:10:00.040 --> 00:10:03.220
I didn't know that, but I know a lot of people get married there.

245
00:10:03.360 --> 00:10:04.800
I've been to a wedding there.

246
00:10:04.980 --> 00:10:05.820
You know what?

247
00:10:05.820 --> 00:10:10.680
But Randy Cordray knew about this location because he had been the best man at a wedding

248
00:10:10.680 --> 00:10:11.000
there.

249
00:10:11.180 --> 00:10:15.920
And when the script came up, he remembered it and suggested it to our locations manager,

250
00:10:16.100 --> 00:10:16.820
Kyle Alexander.

251
00:10:17.180 --> 00:10:20.080
And they scouted it, and they all agreed it was perfect.

252
00:10:20.600 --> 00:10:26.160
So now, Angela, you mentioned how Ken Kwapis said this was a logistically difficult shoot.

253
00:10:26.400 --> 00:10:26.660
Mm-hmm.

254
00:10:27.300 --> 00:10:32.800
Randy said this was a very difficult episode to pull off logistically from a production side

255
00:10:32.800 --> 00:10:35.340
because we had so many people.

256
00:10:35.580 --> 00:10:38.180
So many people, and we were in the middle of a canyon.

257
00:10:38.460 --> 00:10:42.720
He said we had to hire additional crew, including multiple medics.

258
00:10:43.020 --> 00:10:43.360
Mm.

259
00:10:43.360 --> 00:10:47.360
And we had hydration stations and sunscreen stations.

260
00:10:47.760 --> 00:10:52.100
And Sergio and Alan, our caterers, they had to triple their staff.

261
00:10:52.260 --> 00:10:56.600
They had three lines to serve lunch, like three buffet lines.

262
00:10:56.600 --> 00:11:03.660
And then Peter and Vartan at craft service, they also had three stations instead of one.

263
00:11:03.840 --> 00:11:09.680
It was a lot of concern about being able to feed everybody because all the background food,

264
00:11:10.110 --> 00:11:11.500
people were told not to eat.

265
00:11:11.680 --> 00:11:12.820
Yeah, it was fakie food.

266
00:11:12.820 --> 00:11:18.860
It was real food, but it had been at various temperatures that made it sort of unsafe to eat.

267
00:11:19.000 --> 00:11:24.620
So if you watch the episode, you might notice no one eats anything off of their plates of food.

268
00:11:25.020 --> 00:11:25.220
Hmm.

269
00:11:25.400 --> 00:11:26.000
Check it out.

270
00:11:26.380 --> 00:11:29.300
Ken said there was probably like 250 people there.

271
00:11:29.600 --> 00:11:32.060
Guess what my fast fact number three is, lady?

272
00:11:32.280 --> 00:11:32.540
What?

273
00:11:33.000 --> 00:11:33.980
I'll tell you.

274
00:11:34.440 --> 00:11:42.460
We had 14 guest actors, our regular cast, and 185 background performers for this episode.

275
00:11:43.220 --> 00:11:44.760
Also, I thought this was interesting.

276
00:11:45.160 --> 00:11:51.160
Some of the background performers were called special business performers.

277
00:11:51.520 --> 00:11:52.260
Special business?

278
00:11:52.620 --> 00:11:54.340
You know what their special business was?

279
00:11:54.640 --> 00:11:55.200
Volleyball?

280
00:11:55.580 --> 00:11:55.800
Yeah.

281
00:11:56.280 --> 00:11:58.140
Playing volleyball for hours on end.

282
00:11:58.600 --> 00:12:00.920
Lady, were you ever a background performer?

283
00:12:01.220 --> 00:12:02.000
You know it.

284
00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:03.520
That's how I earned my SAG card.

285
00:12:03.660 --> 00:12:04.640
Yeah, same.

286
00:12:05.000 --> 00:12:08.820
Did you have any special business listed on your background performer resume?

287
00:12:09.260 --> 00:12:10.020
Yes, I did.

288
00:12:10.180 --> 00:12:11.440
What was your special business?

289
00:12:11.440 --> 00:12:13.140
That I could speak Indonesian.

290
00:12:13.740 --> 00:12:14.240
Oh.

291
00:12:14.500 --> 00:12:14.880
Mm-hmm.

292
00:12:15.180 --> 00:12:21.280
My special business was water skiing, bowling, and animal handling.

293
00:12:21.560 --> 00:12:24.680
Oh, I also put I could play tennis and roller skate.

294
00:12:24.820 --> 00:12:24.980
Yeah.

295
00:12:24.980 --> 00:12:25.840
Those are a big deal.

296
00:12:25.920 --> 00:12:26.900
Yeah, that's a big deal.

297
00:12:27.280 --> 00:12:28.880
I was on 90210.

298
00:12:29.260 --> 00:12:31.240
Did you have to do any of your special business?

299
00:12:31.520 --> 00:12:32.080
I didn't.

300
00:12:32.080 --> 00:12:38.220
I was one of Tori Spelling's sorority sisters, but I did pass the ballots of a voting, a sorority

301
00:12:38.220 --> 00:12:42.820
voting session to, I handed them to Tori.

302
00:12:43.020 --> 00:12:45.100
Oh, you got to interact.

303
00:12:45.440 --> 00:12:45.660
Yes.

304
00:12:45.860 --> 00:12:46.320
Briefly.

305
00:12:46.320 --> 00:12:47.040
I said nothing.

306
00:12:47.240 --> 00:12:48.540
I just handed some paper to her.

307
00:12:48.800 --> 00:12:56.080
I was an extra on the movie Pleasantville starring Reese Witherspoon and Tobey Maguire, and I had to play a teenager.

308
00:12:56.240 --> 00:12:59.960
And that was really fun because I got to dress up in old-timey clothes the whole time.

309
00:13:00.700 --> 00:13:02.080
But check this out.

310
00:13:02.300 --> 00:13:10.120
They came into the extras holding area because they needed three couples to make out in the backseat of a car.

311
00:13:10.260 --> 00:13:16.500
They were going to, like, pan across all these different couples making out until they got to the car with the lead actors.

312
00:13:16.880 --> 00:13:18.300
Were you part of the make-out couples?

313
00:13:18.460 --> 00:13:18.680
No.

314
00:13:18.840 --> 00:13:24.620
You would have become a featured extra if you did it and you got a bump in pay, but I felt too shy, so I didn't volunteer.

315
00:13:24.620 --> 00:13:36.860
But, lady, we had a very special background performer on this episode of The Office, and it was Jenny Tan who runs the fan website OfficeTelly.com.

316
00:13:37.120 --> 00:13:37.720
Yes.

317
00:13:37.980 --> 00:13:48.020
We had all become friends with her, and she was invited to spend the entire week with us getting an exclusive look at the finale, and they asked her to be an extra.

318
00:13:48.020 --> 00:13:54.960
And, lady, she documented all five days of shooting this episode on OfficeTelly.com.

319
00:13:55.060 --> 00:13:58.620
It is still there, and she has tons of photos.

320
00:13:59.240 --> 00:13:59.840
Amazing.

321
00:14:00.320 --> 00:14:06.920
She also gave this amazing breakdown of what it was like to be a background performer on Company Picnic.

322
00:14:07.140 --> 00:14:07.980
Oh, my gosh.

323
00:14:08.000 --> 00:14:08.740
Are you going to share?

324
00:14:08.900 --> 00:14:09.280
Yes.

325
00:14:09.560 --> 00:14:16.420
You guys, Jenny was sort of someone who made a community for The Office online before anyone was doing that.

326
00:14:16.480 --> 00:14:17.500
Before social media.

327
00:14:17.500 --> 00:14:28.920
Before social media, and she was there for the show, providing this community in our earliest days when we didn't even know the future of the show, and it meant a lot to all of us.

328
00:14:29.140 --> 00:14:37.300
And we would reach out to her and give her, like, exclusive tips or content or photos that she would post on the website, and it became kind of the hub.

329
00:14:37.680 --> 00:14:46.720
Yeah, and this was before networks did any kind of social media or website for any of their shows, and our show had this presence because of Jenny.

330
00:14:46.720 --> 00:14:54.220
And so her documenting of our show is very extensive, and it's all still there on OfficeTelly.com.

331
00:14:54.300 --> 00:14:55.680
There's all these exclusive interviews.

332
00:14:55.840 --> 00:14:56.360
It's amazing.

333
00:14:56.740 --> 00:15:07.780
She said you could really tell the people who were professional background artists because they would bring tons of gear, like rolling luggage filled with what she said, quote,

334
00:15:08.020 --> 00:15:10.540
outerwear for every possible weather condition.

335
00:15:11.920 --> 00:15:15.080
Chairs, water, snacks, something to read.

336
00:15:15.700 --> 00:15:22.000
It's like, you know, when you go to the beach and you see people, like, come with the wagon full of stuff or at the soccer game?

337
00:15:22.300 --> 00:15:24.240
That's a professional background performer.

338
00:15:24.500 --> 00:15:24.900
Oh, yeah.

339
00:15:24.900 --> 00:15:29.700
I remember doing background work, and there was a guy that had a backpack that was a chair.

340
00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:30.440
Yes.

341
00:15:30.560 --> 00:15:34.640
And you could open the backpack, and there was stuff inside, or you could unfold it and sit in it.

342
00:15:34.720 --> 00:15:36.980
Because background performers do not get trailers.

343
00:15:37.320 --> 00:15:45.220
You mostly are going to be directed to a holding area with some folding chairs in it, but my experience was there were never enough chairs for each person.

344
00:15:45.340 --> 00:15:47.360
Never enough chairs, and they're usually outside.

345
00:15:47.360 --> 00:15:48.000
Yeah.

346
00:15:48.680 --> 00:15:54.880
So Jenny said that when she first went to the wardrobe tent, she was given an apron to wear, and she said she would be a grill cook.

347
00:15:55.020 --> 00:16:04.700
But then about an hour later, production pulled her aside and made her a Buffalo branch employee, and then they winked and said, you're going to be part of a special branch.

348
00:16:04.900 --> 00:16:06.520
But she didn't know what that meant yet.

349
00:16:06.520 --> 00:16:17.680
And get this, Jenny talked to a bunch of the background folks, and she said that most of them were not Office fans, that they had never seen the show,

350
00:16:17.740 --> 00:16:20.620
and that a few of them did not know who Steve Carell was.

351
00:16:20.840 --> 00:16:30.700
She said they were thrilled to be working on our show because we had a reputation for treating background performers very, very well and for providing great food.

352
00:16:30.700 --> 00:16:39.060
She said one person told her, from a work perspective, to be able to be an extra on the office for an entire week, I feel like I won the lottery.

353
00:16:39.300 --> 00:16:42.140
So that made me really proud that our show did a good job.

354
00:16:42.260 --> 00:16:47.920
Because as a person who did a lot of background work, yeah, it meant a lot.

355
00:16:48.260 --> 00:16:55.120
Usually the background performers were like the last people who get to go through the lunch line, and it's like what's left, almost nothing,

356
00:16:55.380 --> 00:16:57.820
and you were not allowed to go to craft services.

357
00:16:57.820 --> 00:17:07.740
But on our show, we had a whole buffet set aside just for the background performers and a whole craft services table that they kept full.

358
00:17:07.920 --> 00:17:09.040
And it was awesome.

359
00:17:09.460 --> 00:17:11.000
Well, you know, that's Randy Cordray.

360
00:17:11.880 --> 00:17:13.540
Randy took care of everyone.

361
00:17:13.960 --> 00:17:17.720
However you were a part of our show, he was going to make sure you were taken care of.

362
00:17:18.000 --> 00:17:19.480
That is absolutely true, lady.

363
00:17:20.060 --> 00:17:22.300
All right, lady, those are all my fast facts.

364
00:17:22.460 --> 00:17:23.220
I mean, it's a lot.

365
00:17:23.520 --> 00:17:24.920
Well, it's a huge episode.

366
00:17:25.120 --> 00:17:25.520
I know.

367
00:17:25.880 --> 00:17:27.620
All right, should we take a break?

368
00:17:27.820 --> 00:17:30.540
And then start breaking down this episode?

369
00:17:30.900 --> 00:17:31.440
Just heads up.

370
00:17:31.440 --> 00:17:32.420
It's going to be a long one.

371
00:17:32.700 --> 00:17:33.700
That's what she said.

372
00:17:33.860 --> 00:17:46.920
We are back, and the phone is ringing at reception.

373
00:17:47.240 --> 00:17:48.560
The lights are out.

374
00:17:48.880 --> 00:17:49.220
Yeah.

375
00:17:49.640 --> 00:17:54.160
Erin takes the call, and she says that Michael is not in right now.

376
00:17:54.560 --> 00:17:57.400
But he'll call this person back when he gets back.

377
00:17:57.520 --> 00:17:58.180
When he wakes up.

378
00:17:58.260 --> 00:17:58.880
I mean, gets back.

379
00:17:59.000 --> 00:17:59.180
Right.

380
00:17:59.760 --> 00:18:00.500
Well, guess what?

381
00:18:00.740 --> 00:18:03.380
Michael is sleeping because he had chicken pot pie for lunch.

382
00:18:03.380 --> 00:18:04.560
Not just chicken pot pie.

383
00:18:04.900 --> 00:18:07.820
Jenna, he had the whole entire chicken pot pie.

384
00:18:07.920 --> 00:18:10.400
A family-sized chicken pot pie.

385
00:18:10.680 --> 00:18:12.820
And he's gone to sleep, as one would do.

386
00:18:13.120 --> 00:18:20.800
Well, the whole office is going to prank Michael into thinking that it is 5 o'clock when he wakes up so that they can all go home early.

387
00:18:21.080 --> 00:18:26.200
And Dwight does not normally condone leaving early, but he does have that appointment with his horse doctor today.

388
00:18:26.200 --> 00:18:27.060
So he's in.

389
00:18:27.960 --> 00:18:29.840
Fan catch from Marcy G.

390
00:18:30.280 --> 00:18:30.820
in Connecticut.

391
00:18:31.260 --> 00:18:34.380
There is a small detail that tickles me every time I see it.

392
00:18:34.520 --> 00:18:40.940
In the cold open, when they're tricking a sleeping Michael into thinking it's 5 p.m., they have to change the time on his watch.

393
00:18:41.240 --> 00:18:44.440
Pam has her shoes off so she doesn't make noise in his office.

394
00:18:44.740 --> 00:18:51.680
But if you notice, Dwight also has his shoes off while he runs outside to change the time on Michael's car.

395
00:18:51.880 --> 00:18:52.740
I saw that.

396
00:18:53.020 --> 00:18:54.600
Jim also has his shoes off.

397
00:18:54.640 --> 00:18:54.920
Yes.

398
00:18:54.920 --> 00:18:55.480
I don't know.

399
00:18:55.560 --> 00:18:57.480
Did everyone just take their shoes off?

400
00:18:58.060 --> 00:19:02.020
We had another fan catch from Lloyd C.

401
00:19:02.320 --> 00:19:04.700
from Australia who said,

402
00:19:04.840 --> 00:19:12.120
During Jim's talking head, when he goes to turn the clock forward, the time starts on 12.50.

403
00:19:12.400 --> 00:19:18.680
But when Dwight goes to Michael's car to change the time, it starts at 12.10.

404
00:19:18.680 --> 00:19:21.560
He said, Was this a continuity gap?

405
00:19:21.700 --> 00:19:25.580
Or did Dwight's panther speed turn time backwards outside of the office?

406
00:19:25.980 --> 00:19:28.540
I think it was probably just a continuity catch.

407
00:19:28.540 --> 00:19:36.560
You know, maybe they had originally intended to see Dwight running outside to the car first and then have Jim explain it in his talking head.

408
00:19:36.740 --> 00:19:38.920
And then maybe they just swapped it in editing.

409
00:19:39.200 --> 00:19:42.380
I bet that's right because Veda would have been all on top of this.

410
00:19:42.380 --> 00:19:45.340
She had everything meticulously time-coded.

411
00:19:45.400 --> 00:19:48.980
Every time we had a clock, she was very, like, particular.

412
00:19:49.260 --> 00:19:51.100
So I feel like that was a change up in editing.

413
00:19:51.380 --> 00:20:03.320
Jenny Tan, who was there that day watching us film, noted that John had a really hard time winding the clock backwards and delivering his monologue and timing it out perfectly.

414
00:20:03.320 --> 00:20:05.280
I thought so watching it.

415
00:20:05.540 --> 00:20:06.820
I'll tell you why.

416
00:20:07.540 --> 00:20:09.500
John is so effortless as Jim.

417
00:20:09.760 --> 00:20:14.500
But in this moment, I could see him concentrating on that clock.

418
00:20:14.680 --> 00:20:17.100
And just for a split second, I thought I saw John.

419
00:20:17.580 --> 00:20:24.440
Well, she said that the knob on the back was really, really tiny and that John kept overshooting the time.

420
00:20:24.640 --> 00:20:29.820
Like, it kept ending on, like, 5.15 or 5.10 instead of right before 5.

421
00:20:30.360 --> 00:20:35.000
But she said they finally got it perfect on the last take, and then they stopped.

422
00:20:35.220 --> 00:20:36.180
They were like, we got it.

423
00:20:36.300 --> 00:20:36.820
Yeah, done.

424
00:20:37.080 --> 00:20:43.100
Well, this whole elaborate ruse ends when they all collectively, on the count of three, just erupt into laughter.

425
00:20:43.540 --> 00:20:44.940
And this wakes Michael up.

426
00:20:45.080 --> 00:20:47.800
He laughs, too, because he always wants to be in on the joke.

427
00:20:47.860 --> 00:20:48.700
Yes, yes.

428
00:20:48.740 --> 00:20:49.840
But you had to be there.

429
00:20:50.020 --> 00:20:50.780
You had to be there.

430
00:20:50.920 --> 00:20:52.280
It was a geography joke.

431
00:20:52.500 --> 00:20:55.520
And then Michael sees the clock and does exactly what they hoped for.

432
00:20:55.600 --> 00:20:56.800
He's like, well, let's all go home.

433
00:20:56.800 --> 00:21:02.640
I had a teeny tiny catch on Pam's desk at 1 minute 33 seconds when we're all laughing.

434
00:21:03.320 --> 00:21:12.060
I noticed, and I had forgotten this, that she has a Pratt mug on her desk that is what is holding all of her pens and pencils.

435
00:21:12.400 --> 00:21:14.980
And then she has her pink mug that she's drinking out of.

436
00:21:15.240 --> 00:21:17.320
But I spotted that Pratt mug.

437
00:21:17.520 --> 00:21:17.780
Aw.

438
00:21:17.780 --> 00:21:20.600
That's a great detail from our set decoration crew.

439
00:21:20.860 --> 00:21:21.820
Well, I love that.

440
00:21:21.820 --> 00:21:28.540
I was way in the corner, back by Stanley's file cabinet, and that was just trying to get me in the shot.

441
00:21:28.800 --> 00:21:30.440
Ken Quapis had me come out from accounting.

442
00:21:30.600 --> 00:21:31.660
Once again, I'm standing.

443
00:21:31.960 --> 00:21:32.220
Mm-hmm.

444
00:21:32.620 --> 00:21:34.400
And that's how we could all be there.

445
00:21:34.700 --> 00:21:38.280
Well, now we have our super short opening credits.

446
00:21:38.600 --> 00:21:40.300
They are, like, 10 seconds.

447
00:21:40.760 --> 00:21:46.560
Jen Salata did an interview with Office Tally, and she said they cut the credits down to get more time for the episode.

448
00:21:46.560 --> 00:21:53.900
She mentioned that they had gone back and forth about maybe making this episode an hour, but she said they didn't feel like they needed it.

449
00:21:54.140 --> 00:22:02.040
There were other season finales, like The Job or Goodbye, Toby, where they had, like, tons of plot points to wrap up and huge moves they were going to make.

450
00:22:02.260 --> 00:22:06.560
But in this episode, she said they didn't have that much.

451
00:22:06.560 --> 00:22:12.840
They just had kind of the one big move with Jim and Pam and a little bit of a move for Michael and Holly.

452
00:22:13.100 --> 00:22:15.820
And then the rest of us, just little nods to our storylines.

453
00:22:15.820 --> 00:22:20.320
Yeah, but she said they did need to steal that little bit of time from the credits.

454
00:22:20.640 --> 00:22:21.900
The day has arrived.

455
00:22:21.900 --> 00:22:24.540
It is the Dunder Mifflin Company Picnic.

456
00:22:24.960 --> 00:22:28.780
Here's a breakdown of all the different branches and their t-shirts.

457
00:22:29.100 --> 00:22:29.520
Oh!

458
00:22:29.860 --> 00:22:31.040
Thank you, Dunderpedia.

459
00:22:31.180 --> 00:22:33.600
I started making notes, and then I saw you had it.

460
00:22:34.440 --> 00:22:36.480
So I'm going to read what you have.

461
00:22:36.920 --> 00:22:38.680
Corporate, black t-shirts.

462
00:22:39.300 --> 00:22:40.180
Akron, yellow.

463
00:22:40.540 --> 00:22:41.620
Albany, light blue.

464
00:22:41.840 --> 00:22:42.680
Buffalo, orange.

465
00:22:43.080 --> 00:22:43.940
Nashua, green.

466
00:22:43.940 --> 00:22:45.840
Rochester, dark purple.

467
00:22:46.300 --> 00:22:47.200
Scranton, red.

468
00:22:47.440 --> 00:22:48.740
And Utica, dark blue.

469
00:22:49.300 --> 00:22:52.700
Lady, I'm calling this episode The Plain Hats Club.

470
00:22:53.100 --> 00:22:59.180
Oh, I can't wait to hear this, because I have some lingo I made up for this episode as well.

471
00:22:59.320 --> 00:23:01.980
Everyone in this episode is wearing a plain hat.

472
00:23:02.180 --> 00:23:06.300
And that is because if you wear a logo anywhere on your shirt or your hat,

473
00:23:06.300 --> 00:23:09.340
the production has to get it cleared with that company firSt.

474
00:23:09.520 --> 00:23:13.280
So I have a breakdown of everyone's plain hat.

475
00:23:13.500 --> 00:23:15.320
Like the plain baseball hats?

476
00:23:15.500 --> 00:23:15.780
Yes!

477
00:23:16.440 --> 00:23:19.460
Jim is wearing a plain black baseball hat.

478
00:23:19.620 --> 00:23:21.140
Pam has a white visor.

479
00:23:21.640 --> 00:23:26.980
Dwight has a tan fishing hat, which I googled and I learned is called a bucket hat.

480
00:23:27.360 --> 00:23:27.620
Hmm.

481
00:23:27.620 --> 00:23:30.820
Kevin is also wearing a tan bucket hat.

482
00:23:31.220 --> 00:23:33.460
Angela has a floppy sun hat.

483
00:23:34.020 --> 00:23:36.220
Andy has a tan Panama hat.

484
00:23:36.740 --> 00:23:39.900
Aaron has a sort of light purple baseball hat.

485
00:23:40.520 --> 00:23:42.440
Creed has a light tan baseball hat.

486
00:23:42.940 --> 00:23:44.640
Ryan has a white visor.

487
00:23:45.160 --> 00:23:47.340
Stanley has a straw sun hat.

488
00:23:48.160 --> 00:23:53.440
Phyllis has no hat while she's playing volleyball, but she has a straw sun hat on the sidelines.

489
00:23:54.000 --> 00:23:55.220
Meredith has no hat.

490
00:23:55.820 --> 00:23:57.280
Oscar has no hat.

491
00:23:58.000 --> 00:24:02.660
Charles Minor has a black Nike visor.

492
00:24:02.840 --> 00:24:04.140
How did we get away with that?

493
00:24:04.140 --> 00:24:05.120
I don't know!

494
00:24:05.400 --> 00:24:06.120
Because it's Idris.

495
00:24:06.320 --> 00:24:08.780
And we're just like, let Idris do what he wants to do.

496
00:24:09.120 --> 00:24:10.580
We're just glad to have you back.

497
00:24:10.780 --> 00:24:11.640
I don't know.

498
00:24:12.600 --> 00:24:15.760
Toby also has a yellow baseball hat with a logo.

499
00:24:15.860 --> 00:24:18.520
It has little trees, but I couldn't read what it was.

500
00:24:18.720 --> 00:24:22.860
Well, you know, part of the hat strategy was because it got so hot during the day.

501
00:24:23.240 --> 00:24:24.080
We needed it.

502
00:24:24.180 --> 00:24:30.160
We needed it when we were playing volleyball as well to kind of create a shade on our faces

503
00:24:30.160 --> 00:24:35.340
so that you could see our expressions so we weren't completely blown out by the sun.

504
00:24:35.520 --> 00:24:36.840
Also for sun protection.

505
00:24:37.160 --> 00:24:40.520
But you might notice that Michael and Holly do not wear hats.

506
00:24:40.720 --> 00:24:46.280
They are mostly in their cutesy little, like, love nest, shady location.

507
00:24:46.620 --> 00:24:47.280
Yeah, exactly.

508
00:24:47.640 --> 00:24:49.560
Well, Jenna, I also did a little bit of tracking.

509
00:24:49.560 --> 00:24:55.380
I want you to know that I feel like this episode really brings out some of our characters' other traits.

510
00:24:55.780 --> 00:24:56.280
Oh.

511
00:24:56.480 --> 00:24:57.300
I made a liSt.

512
00:24:57.600 --> 00:25:10.880
We have Sporty Pam, Saucy Jim, Petty Charles, Trash-Talking Andy, Snarky Michael, and my most favorite, Vacation Stanley.

513
00:25:11.160 --> 00:25:13.560
I love Vacation Stanley.

514
00:25:14.460 --> 00:25:16.920
So the picnic starts with Pam and Jim.

515
00:25:17.120 --> 00:25:22.940
They're walking up, and they share a little bit about what last year's softball company picnic was like.

516
00:25:23.220 --> 00:25:27.080
Jim had that spider in his mitt, but that's not what Pam is talking about.

517
00:25:27.120 --> 00:25:31.240
She's talking about the guy who grabbed her boobs to keep his balance.

518
00:25:31.720 --> 00:25:32.420
Uh-huh.

519
00:25:32.420 --> 00:25:37.500
And then Pam points at her chest and says, yeah, you don't grab these for balance.

520
00:25:37.720 --> 00:25:39.540
And Jim goes, well.

521
00:25:39.900 --> 00:25:41.860
And this is Saucy Jim.

522
00:25:43.920 --> 00:25:48.500
Well, next up, Dwight arrives with his friend, Rolf.

523
00:25:48.700 --> 00:25:50.180
His best friend.

524
00:25:50.680 --> 00:25:58.380
Who they met at a shoe store when Dwight overheard Rolf asking for a shoe that would increase his speed but not leave any tracks.

525
00:25:58.700 --> 00:26:00.220
These two are made for each other.

526
00:26:00.220 --> 00:26:02.700
We had a fan question from David H.

527
00:26:02.800 --> 00:26:03.420
in Pennsylvania.

528
00:26:03.760 --> 00:26:05.160
Who played Dwight's friend?

529
00:26:05.460 --> 00:26:07.740
Was he somehow related to the show already?

530
00:26:08.320 --> 00:26:12.280
Guys, Rolf was played by the amazing James Urbaniac.

531
00:26:12.440 --> 00:26:13.600
And get this, Ange.

532
00:26:13.680 --> 00:26:13.900
What?

533
00:26:14.140 --> 00:26:17.620
He auditioned and was called back for the role of Dwight.

534
00:26:17.980 --> 00:26:20.200
Well, that makes perfect sense to me.

535
00:26:20.240 --> 00:26:20.780
Doesn't it?

536
00:26:21.020 --> 00:26:23.980
He and Rain knew each other from their New York theater days.

537
00:26:23.980 --> 00:26:29.360
And after Rain read this script, he called James and gave him a heads up.

538
00:26:29.860 --> 00:26:33.420
And he told Greg, you need to bring in James Urbaniac for this.

539
00:26:33.540 --> 00:26:35.540
Well, they knew him because they had auditioned him.

540
00:26:35.720 --> 00:26:37.680
So he went in to see Allison Jones.

541
00:26:37.860 --> 00:26:39.140
He read for the role.

542
00:26:39.280 --> 00:26:40.060
And he got the part.

543
00:26:40.680 --> 00:26:45.180
Ken talked a little bit in the DVD commentary about just how perfectly cast James was.

544
00:26:45.180 --> 00:26:51.600
And that for a guest star to come in on such a big episode, James just fit in so seamlessly,

545
00:26:51.620 --> 00:26:54.020
you would have never known he had never been on the show before.

546
00:26:54.220 --> 00:26:57.100
That is just how organic his performance was.

547
00:26:57.240 --> 00:26:59.020
And that he just crushed it.

548
00:26:59.140 --> 00:27:04.040
Well, he's one of those guys who's been a working actor for a very, very long time.

549
00:27:04.240 --> 00:27:07.920
And I feel like he's one of those people where you recognize him

550
00:27:07.920 --> 00:27:10.000
immediately from his body of work.

551
00:27:10.080 --> 00:27:10.620
He's a pro.

552
00:27:10.620 --> 00:27:13.540
So we got this interesting letter from Jana M.

553
00:27:13.760 --> 00:27:16.060
in Westport County, Mayo, Ireland.

554
00:27:16.480 --> 00:27:17.180
Oh, wow.

555
00:27:17.360 --> 00:27:18.840
We have letters from everywhere.

556
00:27:18.980 --> 00:27:20.160
This is so exciting to me.

557
00:27:20.340 --> 00:27:23.780
You know, we added a feature on the website where you can say where you're writing from.

558
00:27:24.100 --> 00:27:24.920
I love that.

559
00:27:25.040 --> 00:27:29.260
I know, me too, because it's really fun to see how international the show is.

560
00:27:29.420 --> 00:27:29.520
I know.

561
00:27:29.600 --> 00:27:33.600
Well, when we get mail here to Earwolf that I open, I can always see where people are from.

562
00:27:33.720 --> 00:27:35.840
But before in emails, we weren't always sure.

563
00:27:36.220 --> 00:27:37.720
Here is what Jana said.

564
00:27:38.020 --> 00:27:38.740
Fun fact.

565
00:27:38.740 --> 00:27:44.360
James Urbaniak, the actor who played Dwight's best friend, Rolf, whom he met in a shoe store,

566
00:27:44.560 --> 00:27:49.280
also played a shoe store employee with a foot fetish on Sex and the City.

567
00:27:49.800 --> 00:27:52.980
What is it about James Urbaniak and shoe stores?

568
00:27:53.460 --> 00:27:54.600
That's really funny.

569
00:27:54.780 --> 00:27:55.580
I think that's fun.

570
00:27:56.000 --> 00:27:57.440
And now Christine B.

571
00:27:57.740 --> 00:27:59.880
from Mississippi asked this.

572
00:28:00.160 --> 00:28:05.600
I've heard that Rolf was introduced because Moe's was busy on Parks and Rec.

573
00:28:05.820 --> 00:28:06.900
Is that true?

574
00:28:06.900 --> 00:28:09.040
Oh, savvy question.

575
00:28:09.440 --> 00:28:10.460
It is true.

576
00:28:10.920 --> 00:28:15.940
You guys, originally, Moe's was supposed to come to the picnic with Dwight.

577
00:28:16.300 --> 00:28:21.540
And they called up Mike Schur, who was now running Parks and Rec.

578
00:28:21.760 --> 00:28:27.320
And the week we shot Company Picnic, he was directing an episode of Parks and Rec as well

579
00:28:27.320 --> 00:28:28.380
as running the show.

580
00:28:28.580 --> 00:28:30.660
And he was like, you guys, I can't do it.

581
00:28:30.660 --> 00:28:33.360
And I guess they begged him, like Jen and Paul.

582
00:28:33.560 --> 00:28:35.540
They were like, please, you have to come be Moe's.

583
00:28:35.580 --> 00:28:38.540
And he's like, you guys, I can't do it.

584
00:28:38.720 --> 00:28:41.040
I am running a whole other show now.

585
00:28:41.180 --> 00:28:41.580
Yes.

586
00:28:42.080 --> 00:28:45.160
And then, you know, we're going to have Mike on the show.

587
00:28:45.340 --> 00:28:46.020
I can't wait.

588
00:28:46.020 --> 00:28:50.700
And he's going to tell us a little bit about how he also truly hated playing Moe's.

589
00:28:51.140 --> 00:28:51.800
Hated it.

590
00:28:51.980 --> 00:28:52.700
Hated it.

591
00:28:52.980 --> 00:29:00.220
So I think he was like really happy that he had a good excuse to not be out in this field

592
00:29:00.220 --> 00:29:02.740
in his wool clothes for four days.

593
00:29:02.920 --> 00:29:07.000
It also made sense to me that Dwight wouldn't have brought Moe's to the Company Picnic.

594
00:29:07.000 --> 00:29:11.460
And I love this twist that he's got this best friend.

595
00:29:11.880 --> 00:29:12.080
Yeah.

596
00:29:12.500 --> 00:29:18.180
You know, there's a deleted talking head that made me laugh, but also sad where Michael says

597
00:29:18.180 --> 00:29:21.000
he usually brings the guy from his condo with him.

598
00:29:21.460 --> 00:29:23.440
But this year he came by himself.

599
00:29:24.660 --> 00:29:26.520
The guy from his condo.

600
00:29:26.920 --> 00:29:27.440
Yeah.

601
00:29:27.960 --> 00:29:31.880
Well, Rolf is going to appear in four episodes of The Office.

602
00:29:31.880 --> 00:29:33.300
This was his firSt.

603
00:29:33.740 --> 00:29:34.940
We loved you, James.

604
00:29:35.140 --> 00:29:36.520
He was really fun to work with.

605
00:29:36.540 --> 00:29:36.920
So fun.

606
00:29:37.860 --> 00:29:39.440
Now enters Holly.

607
00:29:40.280 --> 00:29:43.960
Oh, and Michael sees her and Kevin's like, there's Holly.

608
00:29:44.160 --> 00:29:45.860
And Michael's like, oh, you know, we're just friends.

609
00:29:46.580 --> 00:29:51.120
There's a whole awkward greeting between Holly and her boyfriend, AJ, with Michael.

